### How do you ensure only one instance consumes a message?

The behavior hinges entirely on the group id. In Spring Boot you set it once with `spring.kafka.consumer.group-id=transfer-processor`, and you deploy three instances of the service, all reading this same property. Because they share the group id, Kafka's group coordinator treats them as one group and divides the topic's partitions among them. A record sits in exactly one partition, and that partition is owned by exactly one of the three instances.

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecord;
import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class TransferProcessor {

    // All 3 instances share this group id
    @KafkaListener(topics = "transfers", groupId = "transfer-processor")
    public void onTransfer(ConsumerRecord<String, String> record) {
        System.out.println("Instance " + getInstanceId()
            + " processing transfer: key=" + record.key()
            + " partition=" + record.partition());
    }

    private String getInstanceId() {
        return System.getenv().getOrDefault("INSTANCE_ID", "local");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output (3 instances, 3 partitions):
Instance A processing transfer: key=SAV2001 partition=2
-> Only Instance A sees this message (partition 2 assigned to A)
-> Instance B and C never see it
```

### How does partition assignment work with three instances?

Suppose the topic `transfers` has three partitions and you run three instances with the same group id. Kafka assigns partition 0 to instance A, partition 1 to instance B, and partition 2 to instance C. When the producing service sends a transfer keyed by account id SAV2001, Kafka hashes the key to partition 2, so only instance C consumes it.

```java
package com.bank.assignment;

public class ThreeInstanceAssignment {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Topic: transfers (3 partitions)");
        System.out.println("Group: transfer-processor (3 instances)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Assignment:");
        System.out.println("  Instance A -> Partition 0");
        System.out.println("  Instance B -> Partition 1");
        System.out.println("  Instance C -> Partition 2");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Producer sends: key=SAV2001");
        System.out.println("  hash(SAV2001) % 3 = 2");
        System.out.println("  -> Partition 2 -> Instance C processes it");
        System.out.println("  -> Instances A and B never see this message");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Instance C processes transfer for SAV2001
Instances A and B never see it
```

### What is the common mistake with group ids?

The most dangerous mistake is letting each instance generate a unique group id, for example by appending the hostname or a random suffix. That turns each instance into its own group, and since every group receives all partitions, all three instances consume the same message.

```java
package com.bank.mistakes;

import java.util.UUID;

// WRONG: Each instance generates a unique group id.
// All three instances process every message.
public class WrongGroupId {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String instanceA = "transfer-processor-" + UUID.randomUUID();
        String instanceB = "transfer-processor-" + UUID.randomUUID();
        String instanceC = "transfer-processor-" + UUID.randomUUID();

        System.out.println("WRONG group ids:");
        System.out.println("  Instance A: " + instanceA);
        System.out.println("  Instance B: " + instanceB);
        System.out.println("  Instance C: " + instanceC);
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Each instance is its OWN group!");
        System.out.println("All three process EVERY message.");
        System.out.println("Result: Rajesh debited THREE times!");
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.mistakes;

// CORRECT: All instances use the same group id.
public class CorrectGroupId {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String groupId = "transfer-processor";

        System.out.println("CORRECT group id: " + groupId);
        System.out.println("  Instance A: " + groupId);
        System.out.println("  Instance B: " + groupId);
        System.out.println("  Instance C: " + groupId);
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("All three share ONE group.");
        System.out.println("Each message processed by exactly ONE instance.");
        System.out.println("Result: Rajesh debited exactly ONCE.");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: unique group ids = triple processing = triple debit
CORRECT: shared group id = single processing = one debit
```

### What happens if there are fewer partitions than instances?

If the topic has only one partition but you run three instances in the same group, only one instance will ever be active because a partition can be owned by just one consumer. The other two sit idle as hot standbys. They still provide failover: if the active instance dies, a rebalance hands the partition to one of the idle instances.

```java
package com.bank.scaling;

public class PartitionCountVsInstances {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("1 partition, 3 instances:");
        System.out.println("  Instance A -> Partition 0 (ACTIVE)");
        System.out.println("  Instance B -> (IDLE)");
        System.out.println("  Instance C -> (IDLE)");
        System.out.println("  Only Instance A processes messages");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("If Instance A dies:");
        System.out.println("  Rebalance -> Instance B -> Partition 0 (ACTIVE)");
        System.out.println("  Failover works, but no parallel processing");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("3 partitions, 3 instances:");
        System.out.println("  All three ACTIVE, processing in parallel");
        System.out.println("  Full utilization of all instances");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
1 partition, 3 instances: 1 active, 2 idle
3 partitions, 3 instances: 3 active, 0 idle
```

### How do you handle competing consumers vs broadcast?

For competing consumers where each message is processed once, keep the group id identical across instances. For broadcast where every instance must see every message, give each instance a distinct group id.

```java
package com.bank.patterns;

import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class CompetingVsBroadcast {

    // Competing consumers: each transfer processed by ONE instance
    @KafkaListener(topics = "transfers", groupId = "transfer-processor")
    public void competingConsumer(String event) {
        System.out.println("[competing] Processed once: " + event);
    }

    // Broadcast: each instance gets every config update
    // group id includes instance id for uniqueness
    @KafkaListener(topics = "config-updates",
        groupId = "#{T(java.lang.System).getenv('HOSTNAME')}-config")
    public void broadcastConsumer(String event) {
        System.out.println("[broadcast] All instances see: " + event);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Competing (transfers): one instance processes each transfer
Broadcast (config-updates): all instances receive every config update
```

### Why do you need consumer idempotency even with same group id?

Even within a single group, a rebalance after a crash can cause a message to be redelivered to a different instance if the offset was not committed before the crash. This gives at-least-once delivery. The consumer should be idempotent so that a rare redelivery does not double-debit the account.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class IdempotentTransferProcessor {

    private final TransferRepository transferRepo;

    public IdempotentTransferProcessor(TransferRepository transferRepo) {
        this.transferRepo = transferRepo;
    }

    public void processTransfer(String transferId, String accountId,
                                BigDecimal amount) {
        // Idempotency check: skip if already processed
        if (transferRepo.existsByTransferId(transferId)) {
            System.out.println("Skipping duplicate transfer: " + transferId);
            return;
        }

        // Process the transfer
        transferRepo.save(transferId, accountId, amount);
        System.out.println("Processed transfer: " + transferId
            + " amount=" + amount);
    }
}

interface TransferRepository {
    boolean existsByTransferId(String transferId);
    void save(String transferId, String accountId, BigDecimal amount);
}
```

```text
Expected output (first delivery):
Processed transfer: TRF-001 amount=50000.00

Expected output (redelivery after rebalance):
Skipping duplicate transfer: TRF-001
-> Rajesh never double-debited
```

### What about Spring Boot configuration?

In Spring Boot, the group id is a single property in `application.yml`. Every instance deployed with this configuration shares the group id automatically.

```java
package com.bank.config;

public class SpringBootGroupIdConfig {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("application.yml:");
        System.out.println("spring:");
        System.out.println("  kafka:");
        System.out.println("    consumer:");
        System.out.println("      group-id: transfer-processor");
        System.out.println("      auto-offset-reset: earliest");
        System.out.println("      enable-auto-commit: false");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Deploy 3 instances with same config:");
        System.out.println("  java -jar bank-service.jar --server.port=8081");
        System.out.println("  java -jar bank-service.jar --server.port=8082");
        System.out.println("  java -jar bank-service.jar --server.port=8083");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("All 3 share group-id=transfer-processor");
        System.out.println("Kafka divides partitions automatically");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
application.yml:
spring.kafka.consumer.group-id: transfer-processor

Deploy 3 instances -> all share group-id -> partitions divided
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The senior insight is that same-group-id guarantees single consumption in the normal case, and idempotency guarantees single effect even across the rare redelivery during failover. The combination of shared group id plus idempotent consumer is the standard pattern for financial message processing.

```java
package com.bank.senior;

public class SingleConsumptionSummary {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Ensuring single consumption:");
        System.out.println("1. Same group.id across all instances");
        System.out.println("   -> Kafka assigns each partition to one instance");
        System.out.println("2. Idempotent consumer (unique constraint + check)");
        System.out.println("   -> Protects against rare redelivery");
        System.out.println("3. Sufficient partitions (>= instance count)");
        System.out.println("   -> All instances active (not idle)");
        System.out.println("4. Manual offset commit (enable.auto.commit=false)");
        System.out.println("   -> At-least-once delivery");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Normal case: group id handles it");
        System.out.println("Edge case: idempotency handles redelivery");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Ensuring single consumption:
1. Same group.id -> one instance per partition
2. Idempotent consumer -> no duplicate effects
3. Sufficient partitions -> all instances active
4. Manual offset commit -> at-least-once delivery
```
